---
title: Different fonts across one document
authors: [markdoc-pdf]
---

# Per-element fonts

Every element that draws text takes its own `font_families` list in the
style file. This page sets a different face for each one so you can see
where the boundaries fall — render it and compare against
`fonts.style.toml` side by side.

The document body you are reading now is the **serif** face from
`body_font_families`. Everything that doesn't override it inherits it,
which is why footnotes and table cells match this paragraph while the
heading above is visibly sans.

## What overrides what

Resolution is a two-step chain, and an empty list means "inherit":

1. An element's own `font_families`, if it sets one.
2. Otherwise `body_font_families`.
3. Otherwise the bundled Noto set.

So you pick the document face once and override only where you want a
contrast. Setting no font at all anywhere is still valid — the chain
just bottoms out at the defaults.

### Headings step down, and keep the sans face

This h3 uses the same sans family as the h1 and h2 above it, because
`[heading.h1]` through `[heading.h3]` all name it. `h4`–`h6` in this
style deliberately do *not*, so they inherit the serif body face — a
visible demonstration of inheritance rather than an oversight.

#### An h4 falls back to the body face

Compare this heading with the h3 above it. Same document, same style
file, different font, because this level names none.

## Code keeps its own family

Inline code such as `--style examples/fonts.style.toml` and fenced
blocks both follow `code_font_family`:

```toml
body_font_families = ["Liberation Serif", "DejaVu Serif"]
code_font_family   = "Liberation Mono"
```

Both are the monospace face — inline and block code share one setting
rather than two.

## Callouts carry a font per kind

Each callout kind has its own entry, so a warning can look different
from a note if your house style calls for it.

{% callout type="note" %}
This note uses the sans face set on `[callout_styles.note]`.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="warning" %}
This warning names no font, so it inherits the serif body face. Kinds
are independent of each other.
{% /callout %}

## Everything else

Lists, footnotes and tables inherit the body face here:

- A list item in the body face.
- Markers themselves are configurable via `[list_marker]`.

| Element | Where the font is set |
| --- | --- |
| Headings | `[heading.h1]` … `[heading.h6]` |
| Code | `code_font_family` |
| Callouts | `[callout_styles.<kind>]` |
| Header / footer | `[page_decoration.header]` / `.footer` |

A footnote inherits the body face too.[^1]

[^1]: Set `[footnote] font_families` if you want it to differ.

The running header and footer on every page use the sans face from
`[page_decoration.header]` and `.footer` — check the top and bottom of
the page rather than the body text for those.

## Naming fonts

Any family installed on the system can be named directly; `font_paths`
is only needed for a font you ship alongside the document. Families
that aren't installed are skipped silently, so listing a fallback after
your first choice keeps the document rendering anywhere:

```toml
[heading.h1]
font_families = ["Liberation Sans", "DejaVu Sans", "Noto Sans"]
```

That is also why this example uses the Liberation and DejaVu families —
they ship almost everywhere on Linux, so the contrast survives on a
machine that has neither of your brand fonts.
